<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
  <title>Decision-Making Skills in Adolescence</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00054</md:content-id>
    <md:uuid>1eda86a7-8c48-47aa-a6ff-6d28df9526e8</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="learning-objectives" id="sect-00001">
    <title>Learning Objectives</title>
    <para id="para-00001">By the end of this section, you will be able to:</para>
    <list id="list-00001">
    <item>Describe the brain regions that govern adolescent risk perception and decision making</item>
    <item>Describe adolescents’ ability to assess risk</item>
    <item>Show how adolescents’ risk perception and decision making affect their behavior</item>
    </list>
    </section>
    <para id="para-00002">Anita is a sixteen-year-old high school sophomore, and she’s feeling a new sense of optimism about her social life. It’s Friday night, and she’s excited to make the most of her extended curfew, which is now until 11 p.m. When her parents ask about her plans, Anita says she’s planning to go to the movies and maybe hang out at the ice cream shop around the corner. In reality, her friends will pick her up and they will attend a big keg party organized by high school seniors. Anita doesn’t think she’ll drink at the party, but if she does, she’ll need some breath mints before heading home. She’s also hoping that the party is not busted by the cops, and that no accidents happen at the venue.</para>
    <para id="para-00003">It's clear from this scenario that Anita is making a set of decisions that could have lasting effects on her health, safety, legal record, family relationships, and social standing. She weighs the pros and cons of different options, considering both the social and emotional aspects of the situation, like the excitement and nervousness of being at a keg party. Adolescent decision making is complex, largely driven by emotions and pleasure-seeking behaviors, and it often lacks a clear assessment of consequences. In that section we will also discuss developments in the so-called “social brain” as well as <term id="term-00001">mirror neurons</term>, which are responsible for abilities like empathy. Together, these structures give adolescents the growing ability to <term id="term-00002">perspective taking</term>—the ability to think about our own mental states as well as those of others (Blakemore, 2008; Guyer et al., 2016).</para>
    <section id="sect-00002">
    <title>Risk Perception and Decision Making</title>
    <para id="para-00004">Let’s briefly review the brain structures and systems responsible for the cognitive developments of adolescence. The frontal lobe, especially the prefrontal cortex, develops rapidly in early adolescence and through the mid-20s. This region of the brain is our executive control center, responsible for our most sophisticated reasoning abilities and for controlling and modulating activity in other regions of the brain. Its ability to control other areas of the brain is still immature during adolescence.</para>
    <para id="para-00005">The other major brain region responsible for much of adolescent development is the limbic system, a collection of structures active in memory formation, emotion processing, and sensory integration and that also contains our brain’s reward center. The developing reward center’s circuitry is of special interest in adolescents because they are more sensitive to reward cues in their environment than are children or adults (Andersen, 2016). For example, in a study examining the response to monetary rewards, researchers found that adolescents showed significantly greater activation in the nucleus accumbens, a key brain region involved in reward processing, compared to both children and adults (Becker et al., 2023). The nucleus accumbens is connected to both the hippocampus and the amygdala and is considered the brain’s reward center. Developments in this area during adolescence have important behavioral consequences that we will discuss later in the context of adolescent risk-taking.</para>
    <para id="para-00006">Development of the limbic system also makes peer relationships more intriguing to teens and is associated with increased novelty seeking, leading teens to continually seek out new and intriguing experiences that may bring reward (or risk) (Steinberg, 2008). The limbic system develops earlier than the prefrontal cortex, making early and mid-adolescence a particularly vulnerable time for teens because this is the period of the greatest mismatch in the developmental timeline. Recent findings from developmental neuroscience argue that this mismatch creates a window of vulnerability for increased risk taking in adolescence (Andrews et al., 2021; Steinberg, 2008). For instance, a 16-year-old ‘s propensity to skateboard without a helmet or to accept risky dares from friends is not trivial, but it more likely reflects limbic system activation leading to pleasure-seeking behaviors and response to peer pressure than a desire to hurt himself.</para>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00001">
    <para id="para-00007">Adolescence has traditionally been viewed as a time when teens are particularly prone to risky behaviors like drug and alcohol use, unprotected sex, and violence. However, “risk” extends beyond just illegal or unsafe behaviors. A recent report from the National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine suggests that instead of viewing all risk taking as detrimental, it is important to recognize its developmental role and offer adolescents opportunities to engage in healthy risks that foster learning, growth, and well-being. Watch this <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104TeenRisk">video on teens and risk taking</link> to learn more.</para>
    </note>
    <para id="para-00008">Teen decision-making is often characterized by a balance between risk-taking and reward-seeking behaviors. Adolescents tend to be more sensitive to potential rewards and less sensitive to potential risks compared to adults. And, as its most basic level, decision making consists of choosing a course of action from several possibilities (Hardman, 2009; Fischhoff &amp; Broomell, 2020). It requires assessing the levels of risk and reward and weighing the probability of one against the likelihood of the other. However, teens often take more risks than adults, because they weigh risks and rewards differently than adults do (Gillespie, 2019). The developments in brain structures and systems that you’ve learned about have several important consequences for adolescent decision making.</para>
    <para id="para-00009">Using a method called graph theory on MRI data, we can see that from ages 12 to 30, connections between certain brain areas get stronger (black lines get thicker as they age). Some regions also become more connected (that is, the green circles get bigger). These changes help the brain improve in complex thinking and social skills.</para>
    <section id="sect-00003">
    <title>Sensitivity to Rewards</title>
    <para id="para-00010">First, adolescents are unusually sensitive to rewards. The neurotransmitter dopamine helps transmit signals in the reward circuits of our brain. During adolescence, dopamine activity increases significantly in both the frontal lobe and the limbic system structures. Strangely enough, this increase makes rewarding activities less satisfying because the brain already has a relatively larger amount of dopamine. As a result, to experience the same amount of stimulation and reward from an activity that an adult might, adolescents seek out more and more novel experiences (Steinberg, 2008), such as driving while drunk, having unprotected sex, or bungee jumping (Khurana et al., 2015; Spear, 2013). This means that, faced with a variety of activities, teens are more sensitive than adults to the amount of reward they might receive and have a developmentally based preference for seeking out more rewarding activities (<link target-id="table-00001"/>). This wouldn’t be a problem except that many highly rewarding activities also come with many risks. For example, it might be quite a thrill to steal a shopping cart from a grocery store, climb inside it, and have your friends push you down a busy road, but this decision also entails the possibility of sustaining physical injury and getting into trouble with law enforcement. For the developing teenager seeking stimulation and reward, however, riding in the shopping cart may become an irresistible lure (<link target-id="fig-00001"/>.</para>
    <table id="table-00001" summary=" ">
    <title>Adult vs. Adolescent Perspective</title>
    <tgroup cols="2">
    <colspec colnum="1" colname="c1"/>
    <colspec colnum="2" colname="c2"/>
    <thead>
    <row class="header-row" valign="top">
    <entry valign="top" align="center">Adult Perspective</entry>
    <entry valign="top" align="center">Adolescent Perspective</entry>
    </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
    <row valign="top">
    <entry valign="top" align="left">Advocates for controlling impulses and making thoughtful decisions</entry>
    <entry valign="top" align="left">Makes reckless decisions</entry>
    </row>
    <row valign="top">
    <entry valign="top" align="left">Takes longstanding risks into account, cherishing a successful life over instant gratification</entry>
    <entry valign="top" align="left">Prioritizes present social acceptance over worries about future risks</entry>
    </row>
    <row valign="top">
    <entry valign="top" align="left">Practices organized, critical thinking</entry>
    <entry valign="top" align="left">Wants to act promptly and avoid wasting valuable time</entry>
    </row>
    </tbody>
    </tgroup>
    </table>
    <figure id="fig-00001">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!-- <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_09_04_ShopCart.jpg"/>  -->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>Caption placeholder</caption>
    </figure>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00004">
    <title>Interest in the Social World</title>
    <para id="para-00011">A second important influence on risk-taking behavior is teenagers’ heightened interest in the social world. The pubertal changes we’ve discussed have many effects, including increasing the relevance of the hormone oxytocin, a chemical messenger in our body that contributes to the formation of social relationships (attachment). Oxytocin is linked to fostering peer interactions and social support among teenagers. Higher levels of oxytocin have been associated with greater trust, cooperation, and prosocial behavior towards peers (for e.g., He et al., 2018). Variable levels of oxytocin in adolescence, along with rapid development of the brain’s social and emotional processing areas, means that social features of the environment take on greater importance and impact in adolescent decision making (Anderson, 2023; Gillespie, 2019). In fact, approval from peers becomes as meaningful as any type of nonsocial reward, such as money or a favorite food. When we combine this social influence with the desire to seek greater sources of reward, we can begin to understand why teens make the decisions they do (Steinberg, 2008). For instance, a teenager decides to join their friends for a weekend getaway to an amusement park, despite the high costs and the loss of a significant portion of their savings. Such a decision reflects the teen’s desire to seek greater rewards, such as social acceptance and belonging, even though it involves taking a financial risk.</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00005">
    <title>Complex Reasoning</title>
    <para id="para-00012">Teens take risks not because they’re incapable of complex hypothetical and deductive reasoning; they are perfectly capable of imagining the consequences of various choices they might make. In fact, researchers have found no real differences between adults and adolescents in their perception of the risk level of their choices (Knoll et al., 2017; Ivers et al., 2009). What’s different is that in considering the tradeoffs, adolescents pay more attention to the possible rewards than to the risks, even more so when the rewards have a social element involving peer approval.</para>
    <para id="para-00013">This is a critical distinction to make because it suggests that increasing knowledge of risks through educational programs is not likely to be an effective solution. Adolescents in a classroom setting can think in sophisticated ways about complex situations, carefully weighing the pros and cons (e.g., Steinberg, 2015). It’s when these same adolescents step into a nuanced everyday situation, combined with peer influence, that we need to be concerned.</para>
    <para id="para-00014">Taken together, the developmental changes in brain structure and function associated with early and mid-adolescence, have come to be seen as a sensitive period – a time of heightened vulnerability and sensitivity in development, a vulnerability that fades with developmental time.</para>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00002">
    <para id="para-00015">How risk-taking changes a teenager's brain | Kashfia Rahman: Why do teenagers occasionally make bold and risky decisions? Is it due to a sudden shift towards recklessness, or is it simply a natural phase of development? To explore this, Kashfia Rahman -- winner of the Intel International Science and Engineering Fair and a Harvard freshman -- designed and carried out an experiment to investigate how high school students respond to and adapt to risk, and how this impacts their developing brains. In this <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104TEDRahman">TED talk, Kashfia Rahman shares her findings on risk and decision-making</link> that might help alter our understanding of teenage behavior.</para>
    </note>
    </section>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00006">
    <title>Risky Adolescent Behavior</title>
    <para id="para-00016">Now that we’ve considered the underlying brain development that makes adolescents vulnerable to risky decision making, let’s consider what risky behavior looks like in an everyday context, and how such behavior has changed over the recent past. The YRBSS provides a nationally representative snapshot of a variety of risky behaviors for teens in the United States and has collected this data since 1990. The cross-sectional survey shows that many risky behaviors among youth are decreasing, while others have either stayed the same over time or even increased. We’ll review a sample of such behaviors and trends.</para>
    <section id="sect-00007">
    <title>Teen Delinquency</title>
    <para id="para-00017">Teen delinquency, also known as juvenile offending, is the act of participating in unlawful behavior as a minor or individual younger than the statutory age of majority. The number of high school adolescents engaging in delinquent behaviors has increased dramatically (Mohammad et al., 2016; Osgood, 2023). These behaviors can range from relatively minor offenses such as shoplifting, vandalism, and substance abuse, to more serious crimes such as drug-related offenses, assault, and theft. Lack of parental control, teen-parent conflict, absenteeism, school dropout, poor academic performance, and a disrespectful attitude toward teachers and other school authorities are risk factors for delinquent adolescents (Dong et al., 2015). Furthermore, the culture of poverty, dangerous neighborhoods, and single mother households reflect family structural disadvantages that puts teens from marginalized, minority groups at increased risk of teenage juvenile delinquency (e.g., De Coster et al., 2022).</para>
    <para id="para-00018">Delinquency has several social impacts on adolescents, including increased levels of crime, vandalism, sexual violence, strained relationships, and emotional turmoil. Olanrewaju and Omoponle (2017) found participating in deviant behavior during adolescence can significantly contribute to the rise in early sexual activities, teenage pregnancies, engagement in sexual encounters with non-romantic partners, and diminished self-esteem, among various other outcomes. Additionally, teen delinquents may face social stigma and discrimination, leading to ostracism, marginalization, and difficulties in reintegrating into society. Researched based on the family systems approach and attachment theory has shown that support from the family can be a crucial barrier to delinquent behavior (add a ref). The word claim is awkward and leaves the reader wondering if this claim has been substantiated.</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00008">
    <title>Substance Use and Abuse</title>
    <para id="para-00019">Drug use among youth is a major public health concern since it is associated with several detrimental consequences. Adolescents’ strong inclination toward experimentation and curiosity, their susceptibility to peer pressure and rebellion against authority, and poor self-worth makes them vulnerable to drug abuse (Degenhardtet al., 2016). Both male and female youth across all ethnoracial groups report markedly less use of alcohol during the prior month than in the past– from 39% to 27% for males and 38% to 19% for females, a roughly 50% decline in ten years. Marijuana use shows declines for male youth in all ethnoracial groups (from 26% to 14%) but remains steady for female youth at 18% in 2021. While these trends are largely encouraging, they do show a sizable proportion of youth have consumed alcohol and marijuana.</para>
    <para id="para-00020">The use of drugs and alcohol is associated with increased anxiety among many less-advantaged and culturally and linguistically diverse children (44% identified this as a concern at 10–11 years), although less so once they reached their early teens (37% at 12–13 years). It is possible that as teens grow, they experience more exposure to this behavior via the media and peers, reducing their anxiety about it (Vassallo &amp; Swami, 2018). A study done on substance use among Argentinean adolescents [ages 13–18 years (M = 15.01 years; SD = 1.5 years; 59.5% female] found that teenagers who start drinking early were more likely to engage in heavier alcohol use, experience more episodes of drunkenness, and use more drugs than those who started drinking later (Pilatti et al., 2013). Similarly, a survey of teens in Thailand found a high prevalence of risky behaviors in the 30 days before the survey, including riding a motorcycle without a helmet (72.7%) or riding after drinking alcohol (13.1%), carrying a weapon (7.6%), and participating in fights that resulted in physical harm (14.0%) (Sam-Angsri et al., 2011).</para>
    <para id="para-00021">The CDC reports that 67% of students have consumed alcohol before twelfth grade, around 50% of high school students have burned marijuana, and 40% have admitted to smoking cigarettes (CDC, 2020). These figures are alarming because the earlier teens begin abusing substances, the greater the risk of developing an addiction. You’ll learn more about teen risky behaviors and emotional development in <link document="m00056"/>.</para>
    <para id="para-00022">Use of illicit drugs, which include cocaine, heroin, and methamphetamine, among other drugs, is also reported. In 2021, 15% of female youth reported such use, as did 12% of males. The YRBSS monitors the use of prescription opioids separately and found similar rates of use as for illicit drugs (15% of female youth and 10% of males). From 2017 to 2021, the CDC reports an uptick in opioid use for Asian, Black, and Hispanic youth. The stress and other social factors experienced by minority groups could be a factor in these data (e.g., Wheldon et al., 2023). Outside of the United States, risk factors for alcohol and cannabis use among Thai secondary school youth (early teenagers aged between 12 and 15舁years) included being male, older students, having poor academic performance, not residing with parents, having family members and peers engaged in drug use, as well as experiencing minimal disapproval from friends regarding drug usage (Assanangkornchai et al., 2018).</para>
    <para id="para-00023">A few studies have shown an association between insufficient sleep and unhealthy risk behaviors including alcohol use, tobacco smoking, marijuana use, and abuse of other illicit/prescription drugs (McKnight-Eily et al., 2011; Winsler et al., 2015). Overall, teens who abuse drugs are also reported to have higher rates of physical and mental illness and reduced overall health and well-being (Schulte et al., 2013).</para>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00003">
    <para id="para-00024">In this <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104TEDHanley">TED talk, Megan Hanley talks about how drugs affect teens</link> and shares her personal insights and experiences. As a junior, she will address the misconceptions about alcoholism and addiction.</para>
    </note>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00009">
    <title>Risky Driving Behavior</title>
    <para id="para-00025">The same YRBSS also monitors risky transportation-related behavior. In 2019, 43% of youth in the United States reported they did not always wear a seat belt while riding in a car. Nearly 17% reported having ridden with a driver who had been drinking alcohol, and 5.4% reported having driven when they had been drinking. Finally, texting while driving remains a frequent risky choice, with 39% of teens reporting they have texted (or sent emails) while driving (<link target-id="fig-00002"/>).</para>
    <figure id="fig-00002">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!-- <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_09_04_RiskBehavior.jpg"/>  -->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>Caption placeholder</caption>
    </figure>
    </section>
    </section>
    <section class="summary" id="sect-00010">
    <list id="list-00002">
    <item>Teen risk perception and decision-making are influenced by factors such as brain development, social influences, and individual characteristics. Adolescents often underestimate risks and prioritize short-term gains due to the immaturity of their prefrontal cortex, which is responsible for impulse control and decision-making. Understanding these factors is crucial for developing interventions that promote safe behaviors.</item>
    <item>Adolescents are especially vulnerable to peer pressure and may engage in risky behaviors to gain acceptance or approval from their peers. Sensation-seeking tendencies, coupled with underdeveloped impulse control and risk assessment skills, can lead to reckless driving, delinquent activities, and experimentation with substances during this stage of development. Additionally, environmental factors such as access to drugs, alcohol, and opportunities for delinquent behavior can further exacerbate these risky behaviors among teens.</item>
    </list>
    </section>
